menu "libtracer"

    # Per-transport module selection (#393) — mirrors the core CMake options
    # LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_{UDP,TCP,WS,CAN}. All default y, so a stock node compiles
    # the full transport set (byte-identical to before this knob existed). Turn one
    # OFF to shed that transport's translation units from libtracer.a for a node that
    # never creates a connection of that kind — the RAM-audit "compose the C6 for its
    # role" lever (shed unused transports), with NO in-source feature macros: which
    # transport compiles is decided by which TUs the build selects. A SPEC of a
    # trimmed `kind` then resolves to SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND (the factory is simply absent).

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_UDP
        bool "Build the UDP datagram transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `udp` transport (udp_transport_t + transport_udp.cpp
            + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node that never creates
            a `kind=udp` connection to drop those TUs from the archive.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_TCP
        bool "Build the TCP stream transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the built-in `tcp` transport (tcp_transport_t / tcp_server_transport_t
            + transport_tcp.cpp + its factory glue). ON by default. Turn OFF on a node
            that never creates a `kind=tcp` connection.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        bool "Build the WebSocket transport"
        default y
        help
            Compile the WebSocket plane: the IDF-native CLIENT link
            (esp_ws_client_link_t) on chip targets, the portable pair on the
            `linux` target. ON by default. Turn OFF on a node that neither serves
            nor dials a WebSocket link. The SERVER link is its own option,
            LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER, below.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER
        bool "Build the WebSocket SERVER link (httpd_ws_link_t; needs the bus tier)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        default n
        select LIBTRACER_BUS_LINKS
        select HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
        select HTTPD_WS_PRE_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT if HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
        help
            Compile httpd_ws_link_t, the esp_http_server-backed WebSocket SERVER
            link (e.g. the SPA at /tracer-ws). It serves many inbound sessions
            and names each one, so it is a BUS by construction: this option
            SELECTS LIBTRACER_BUS_LINKS (#1670), and an image that serves
            WebSocket can never be built without the bus tier.
            httpd_ws_link.cpp also static_asserts kBusLinks, so a hand-made
            configuration that got around the select fails at BUILD time.

            OFF by default since v0.17.0 (the lean default): a node that only
            dials, or does not speak WebSocket, carries neither the server TU
            nor the ~2 KB bus tier.

            HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT is SELECTED, not merely depended on (#963). It
            defaults n in ESP-IDF, and httpd_ws_link.cpp needs its native WS
            API — without the select a project that asked for the server would
            get a bare `undefined reference to
            tr::net::httpd_ws_link_t::httpd_ws_link_t(...)` at link time naming
            no cause. The public header stays includable either way (it only
            names httpd_handle_t / httpd_req_t).

            The `select` is what makes httpd_ws_link_t's admission predicate
            (set_admission_cb) reachable: esp_http_server answers the opening
            WebSocket GET itself and, once it has, does NOT call the URI handler
            for it (httpd_uri.c returns before `uri->handler` on the handshake
            branch), so the ONLY point at which a registered handler sees the
            parsed opening GET — method, URI and headers — is the server's
            ws_pre_handshake_cb, and that member exists only behind
            CONFIG_HTTPD_WS_PRE_HANDSHAKE_CB_SUPPORT. It is present at this
            component's ESP-IDF floor (>=5.5.5, see idf_component.yml), so there
            is no second tier behind it. Conditioned on CONFIG_HTTPD_WS_SUPPORT
            because the upstream symbol depends on it and because that same
            config already gates whether httpd_ws_link.cpp compiles at all; a
            build without it selects nothing.

            WHICH plane you get is decided by the TARGET, not by this bool (#947):

              * chip targets get the IDF-NATIVE links only — esp_ws_client_link_t
                (on esp_transport_ws) and, with LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER=y,
                httpd_ws_link_t (adopts your running esp_http_server). ESP-IDF
                WebSocket never uses POSIX sockets: core's portable
                ws_server_transport_t / ws_client_transport_t are the HOST
                implementation and are NOT compiled here. They are not merely
                unreachable on lwIP, they are unusable — their scatter-gather
                egress asks sendmsg for MSG_NOSIGNAL, which lwip_sendmsg rejects
                with EOPNOTSUPP, and every data frame is then silently discarded
                while the handshake and PING/PONG still work.
              * the `linux` (POSIX host) target gets the portable pair, which is
                the right implementation there and the only one available (that
                target has no esp_http_server).

            Consequence on a chip target: no `ws` entry is registered in the
            built-in transport catalog, so a `:children[]` SPEC carrying `kind=ws`
            with no staged link answers SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND. Serve or dial WebSocket
            on silicon by constructing an IDF-native link and handing it to
            transport_vertex_t::provide_link.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER_RX_DRAIN_FRAMES
        int "WebSocket server: frames read per drain before waiting for idle (0 = no limit)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER
        range 0 4096
        default 32 if ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0 && (FREERTOS_UNICORE || ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU1)
        default 0
        help
            How many inbound frames httpd_ws_link_t reads back to back, while its
            core never goes idle, before it stops reading and waits until that
            core's idle task has run once (ADR-0085). The unread bytes stay in
            the socket, the TCP window closes and the peer is throttled by its
            own stack: nothing is dropped, nothing is buffered, and no timer or
            clock is involved. A link that is not saturating its core never
            waits, because the count restarts each time the core idles.

            Binds tr::graph::config_t::kRxDrainFrames. The default keeps the
            longest idle-free stretch near 0.23 s at the ~7 ms per small request
            measured on an ESP32-C6, well inside the 1 s minimum task watchdog.
            0 removes the frame budget; with the byte budget also 0 the link
            reads without pause, which on a core shared with the idle task lets
            a peer that never stops sending starve it.

            A NON-ZERO BUDGET REQUIRES THAT THE CORE IDLES. The wait ends only
            when the idle task runs, so a spent budget makes the httpd task (and
            every session on it) wait for EVERY lower-priority ready task on its
            core to block, not just for the flood to stop. A build that turned
            the idle-task watchdog check off because it runs a low-priority task
            that never blocks would never see the idle task run: the link would
            stop for good after one budget and httpd_stop() would hang behind
            it. That is why both budgets default to 0 unless the task watchdog
            watches the idle task of every core httpd may run on
            (ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0, and CPU1 on a dual-core build).
            Leave both at 0 in such a build.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER_RX_DRAIN_BYTES
        int "WebSocket server: payload bytes read per drain before waiting for idle (0 = no limit)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER
        range 0 1048576
        default 32768 if ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU0 && (FREERTOS_UNICORE || ESP_TASK_WDT_CHECK_IDLE_TASK_CPU1)
        default 0
        help
            The byte half of LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER_RX_DRAIN_FRAMES: a drain ends when
            either budget is spent. Frames bound a flood of small writes; bytes
            bound a flood of large frames. The frame that crosses the line is
            read whole. Binds tr::graph::config_t::kRxDrainBytes. The default is
            the link's own per-frame cap, so one maximum-size frame is one drain.
            Same requirement as the frame budget: 0 unless the core idles.

    # WebSocket CLIENT timing (#1160). These three are the esp_ws_client_link_t recv
    # loop's cadence and its re-dial policy: POLICY, not derivations, and with no
    # per-link RAM behind them — so they are per-IMAGE Kconfig rather than constructor
    # arguments (which is what the RAM-bearing sizes got instead). Every default is the
    # historical literal, so a stock build compiles the values this link always had.
    #
    # The link's OTHER two bounds — the dial timeout and the write timeout — are
    # deliberately NOT here. They are derived from CONFIG_ESP_TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_S, and
    # that derivation is what keeps one stalled peer inside a single watchdog window; a
    # knob over them would let a node be configured into a watchdog panic. Move the
    # watchdog instead. All five are readable at runtime via
    # esp_ws_client_link_t::timing().

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_READ_TIMEOUT_MS
        int "WS client: read timeout after a positive poll (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 60000
        default 100
        help
            How long esp_ws_client_link_t's recv thread waits for bytes that
            esp_transport_poll_read has already reported as readable. Short by
            design — the data is buffered in the stack, so this bounds a stall,
            it does not pace anything. Raise it only on a target whose lwIP
            hands the poll and the data to the recv thread far apart.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_POLL_MS
        int "WS client: recv-loop poll turn (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 10000
        default 200
        help
            One turn of the client recv loop: how long it parks in
            esp_transport_poll_read before re-checking the stop flag. This is the
            teardown-latency knob — a destructor cannot join the recv thread
            faster than one turn — traded against how often an idle link wakes
            the CPU. Lower it for a node that tears links down on a UI action;
            raise it on a battery node where an idle WS client is pure overhead.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_TX_QUEUE_DEPTH
        int "WS client: frames queued behind an in-flight write"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 0 64
        default 2
        help
            How many outbound frames esp_ws_client_link_t may hold while another
            task is already writing to the socket. A sender that finds a write in
            flight copies its frame into one of these slots and returns at once;
            the writing task sends it next. Past the depth the frame is dropped and
            counted in tx_drops — no sender ever waits on another sender's write,
            so a stalled peer cannot stall an unrelated task that publishes toward
            it. Each slot grows to the largest frame queued in it (at most the
            link's tx buffer) the first time it is used and keeps that memory.
            0 queues nothing: a concurrent sender always drops.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_RECONNECT_BACKOFF_MS
        int "WS client: backoff before re-dialing (ms)"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        range 1 600000
        default 1500
        help
            Upper bound on the wait after a failed or lost connection before the
            recv thread dials again. An UPPER bound only: the wait is on a
            condition variable the destructor signals, so a teardown never pays
            it. Raise it for a fleet that must not hammer a peer that is down;
            lower it on a bench node chasing a peer reboot. It is also what a
            refused admission costs before the next dial, so a node whose peer
            gates admission pays this per rejected attempt.

    config LIBTRACER_WS_CLIENT_DIAL_STATS
        bool "WS client: count and log failed dials"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        default n
        help
            Adds dial_attempts and dial_failures to esp_ws_client_link_t::stats_t
            and logs one WARN per failed dial, limited to one a minute (#1606).
            OFF by default: the link then carries no dial counters and a failed
            dial logs nothing. This is a per-image choice, not a per-link one
            (#1662). Turn it on for a node whose operator has to tell "hammering
            an unreachable peer" apart from "never tried".

    config LIBTRACER_LINK_TRAFFIC_STATS
        bool "WS links: count frames and bytes per connection"
        depends on LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_WS
        default n
        help
            Adds the TRAFFIC half of link_counters_t (rx_frames, rx_bytes,
            tx_frames, tx_bytes, last_rx_us) to esp_ws_client_link_t::stats()
            and httpd_ws_link_t::enumerate_peer_stats(). Each delivered or
            written message then takes a lock (the client's counter mutex, the
            server's link-wide peers_m_) and, on receive, reads the timer.
            OFF by default: the fields are absent, no success path takes a lock
            or reads the clock for them, and the client link carries no counter
            mutex. The drop counters and connected_at_us are always kept. This
            is a per-image choice, not a per-link one (#1663).

    config LIBTRACER_BUS_LINKS
        bool "Carry the bus tier (peer-named links, per-peer addressing)"
        default n
        help
            Bind tr::graph::default_config_t::kBusLinks = true: the ADR-0044
            peer-named tier — per-peer addressing, in-band peer enumeration and
            the peer-lifecycle notifiers — that a link serving MANY named peers
            needs (#1670).

            OFF by default since v0.17.0, the lean choice: a point-to-point node
            does not pay for it (measured on rv32: 2,078 B of flash across
            fwd_router.cpp and transport_vertex.cpp, 0 B of .bss). The WS server
            (LIBTRACER_WS_SERVER) SELECTS it, because httpd_ws_link_t is a bus by
            construction; CAN, the other bus by construction, is only offered
            below when this is on. With it off, a peer-named SPEC is refused
            rather than quietly served as a flat link.

    config LIBTRACER_TRANSPORT_CAN
        bool "Build the CAN transport"
        depends on LIBTRACER_BUS_LINKS
        default y
        help
            Compile the CAN transport (transport_can.cpp portable framing/reassembly +
            the TWAI-backed can_link_t on chip targets, SocketCAN on the linux host).
            Needs LIBTRACER_BUS_LINKS (a CAN link is peer-named by construction), and
            is ON by default once that is. Turn OFF on a bus node with no CAN bus —
            this also drops the TWAI driver glue (twai_link.cpp).

    config LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_LINKS
        bool "Build the link-liveness engine (self-healing dial connections)"
        default n
        help
            Compile the RFC-0014 S5 link-liveness engine (self_heal_link.cpp) and
            bind kSelfHealLinks = true: a config-created DIAL connection of a
            built-in kind (udp/tcp/ws) is then minted DORMANT, dials on demand,
            and self-heals with backoff under its own worker thread.

            OFF by default since v0.17.0 (#1670), the lean choice. Measured on an
            ESP32-C6 image: 4,336 B of reachable self_heal_link_t symbols, and
            0 B of .dram0.bss (the engine is code, not a static table). With it
            off the built-in DIAL kinds dial EAGERLY at creation (no redial, no
            backoff), a node staging its links with provide_link is unaffected,
            and registering a custom self_heal_dial kind is REFUSED (the kind is
            not catalogued and a SPEC naming it answers SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND) rather
            than quietly downgraded.

    config LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_WORKER_STACK
        int "Link-liveness worker stack (bytes, 0 = pthread default)"
        range 0 32768
        default 0
        depends on LIBTRACER_SELF_HEAL_LINKS
        help
            Stack size for the link-liveness engine's worker thread, PER ENGINE-
            MANAGED CONNECTION. 0 means the platform default, which on IDF is
            CONFIG_PTHREAD_TASK_STACK_SIZE_DEFAULT taken from the heap for every
            such link — 12,288 B on the reporter's sdkconfig in #1470.

            The worker is the sole dialer and the sole liveness publisher, so its
            stack has to carry a connect() and the publish fan-out beneath it.
            Size it deliberately on a board with tens of KB of free heap. A value
            below the platform floor is ignored and the default stack is used.

    config LIBTRACER_CREATION_HOOKS
        bool "Let a vertex carry a creation hook (opt-in write-creates)"
        default n
        help
            Bind tr::graph::config_t::kCreationHooks = true (RFC-0030 §7.2, #2000):
            a parent vertex can then carry a creation hook, installed with
            graph_t::set_creation_hook, that decides whether a data write to a
            missing child creates it. Use it for an opt-in creation flow, such as
            an OTA-style write that names the image it brings.

            OFF by default, the lean choice (RFC-0030 §18 Q5). Off, no vertex has
            a hook slot, graph_t::set_creation_hook answers SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND, and
            a data write to a missing child is refused with not_found and creates
            nothing. This is a per-image choice.

    config LIBTRACER_ARENA_BYTES
        int "Default root: static arena size (bytes)"
        range 1024 4194304
        default 262144 if IDF_TARGET_LINUX
        default 32768
        help
            Size of the static arena behind libtracer's default root (ADR-0083,
            libtracer #1783). A graph_t built without a source draws its values,
            tables and the net defaults from it, and nothing in libtracer draws
            from the heap for it. It is one .bss array, so the linker map shows
            exactly what libtracer holds by default; it is reserved whether or
            not it is used, and it never shrinks.

            An exhausted arena refuses: a write answers BACKPRESSURE, a
            registration at init stops with a message naming the sub-pool and
            the bytes it needed. Size it against the node's peak, which the
            :stats.mem.values, .tables and .net seams report per sub-pool. An
            application that injects its own block source into every graph_t
            can lower it. With every source injected (graph, router planes,
            transport vertex), nothing draws here (libtracer #2052).

            A UDP link's receive buffers are one byte past the smaller of its
            SPEC's max_frame and its rx backend's slot. A link given a bounded rx
            backend (a pool, as the full_node example's listener is) therefore
            draws a slot-sized scratch from this arena. A link left on the
            default rx backend has no slot bound, so it draws a 64 KiB scratch
            and 64 KiB receive segments here unless its SPEC sets max_frame; on
            a 32 KiB arena those draws are refused and every datagram is dropped,
            counted in the link's dropped_rx. The linux target, which runs the
            examples' host peers on those defaults, defaults to 256 KiB.

            Binds tr::graph::config_t::kArenaBytes.

    config LIBTRACER_VERTEX_LOCK_STRIPES
        int "Vertex lock-stripe count"
        range 1 256
        default 16
        help
            Number of process-wide mutex/condvar lock stripes shared by every
            graph vertex (libtracer #361 section 2). This is the ONLY global
            mutable buffer libtracer links into a node, and its cost has two
            halves that behave differently — independent, either way, of how
            many vertices you register:

              * STATIC RAM, reserved at link time and NOT lazy: N stripe structs
                plus N condvar handles of .bss. On a unicore build the component
                strips their false-sharing padding, so 16 stripes cost 128 B
                rather than 1,024 B (measured, esp32c6 -Os).
              * HEAP, and this half IS lazy: the FreeRTOS mutex behind a stripe's
                handle (~90 B) is created on that stripe's first lock, so a
                stripe no vertex has hashed onto costs no heap at all.

            The stripe a vertex rides is chosen by hashing its pinned address,
            so lowering the count only raises CONTROL-PLANE lock contention
            (ring trim, edge/ACL mutation, await wake); the lock-free LKV
            read/write hot path is unaffected. On a single-core chip target a
            small count (4-8) reclaims RAM at negligible contention cost; the
            host/multi-core default stays 16.

            The value is delivered as the ordinary constexpr
            tr::graph::kVertexLockStripes in a generated <libtracer/config.hpp>
            (ADR-0068) that shadows the checked-in default for every translation
            unit of the build — one shared header, so the stripe table's size can
            never diverge across TUs. (On ESP-IDF the table itself is a guarded
            function-local static, not the host's `inline constinit` one:
            libstdc++ makes std::mutex's constructor constexpr only where its
            gthreads port supports static init, which ESP-IDF's does not.)

endmenu
